menu "Thermo Post"

config THERMOPOST_RUN_BRINGUP_PROBE
    bool "Run the hardware bring-up probe at boot"
    default n
    help
        Runs main/bringup_probe.cpp before the normal boot sequence: identifies
        the SoC, scans I2C, reads the LCD controller ID, blinks the backlight,
        and self-tests storage and settings, ending in a GATE PASS / GATE FAIL
        summary. Turn this on when re-checking the pin map against hardware or
        after rewiring.

        DESTRUCTIVE: it erases the temperature log and resets settings to
        defaults so its checks start from a known state. It also parks the boot
        forever if any check fails, so the app will not come up.

config THERMOPOST_TOUCH_OFFSET_X
    int "Touch X offset, in pixels"
    default 0
    range -40 40
    help
        Added to every reported touch X, in screen coordinates, after the
        controller's swap/mirror transform. Positive moves the reported point
        right.

config THERMOPOST_TOUCH_OFFSET_Y
    int "Touch Y offset, in pixels"
    default -6
    range -40 40
    help
        Added to every reported touch Y, in screen coordinates, after the
        controller's swap/mirror transform. Positive moves the reported point
        DOWN the screen; negative moves it UP.

        Defaults to -6 because taps register slightly below where they are aimed.
        Two things cause that and both push the same way: the touch glass is
        bonded over the LCD with a small vertical offset, and a fingertip's
        contact patch sits below the point the user believes they are pointing
        at.

        Dial it in with THERMOPOST_TOUCH_LOG rather than by feel. If the error
        is roughly the same at the top and the bottom of the screen it is an
        offset and this fixes it; if it grows towards one edge the problem is a
        scale mismatch in the touch config's x_max/y_max instead, and this would
        only hide it in the middle.

config THERMOPOST_TOUCH_LOG
    bool "Log every touch point"
    default n
    help
        Logs the raw controller reading and the adjusted screen coordinate on
        every press, so a known target can be tapped and the difference read off.
        Presses are human-paced, so this costs nothing while it is on.

# --- Audio ------------------------------------------------------------------
# Three of these exist because the LCDwiki page is the only source for the codec
# wiring and it does not say whose point of view its signal names are from. Each
# is a coin flip that sounds identical when it is wrong — silence — so they are
# switches to bisect with rather than edits to guess at.

config THERMOPOST_AUDIO_SWAP_DATA_PINS
    bool "Swap the I2S data pins (DOUT <-> DIN)"
    default n
    help
        board_pins.hpp reads the wiki's "DOUT=6 / DIN=8" from the ESP32's point
        of view: IO6 carries samples to the codec. If the page meant the CODEC's
        point of view instead, the two are backwards and playback is being sent
        to the microphone pin — the codec configures fine over I2C, the amplifier
        enables, and nothing comes out.

        Turn this on to send playback on IO8 instead. If the speaker works either
        way, leave it off; if it only works on, fix board_pins.hpp and retire
        this option.

config THERMOPOST_AUDIO_PA_INVERT
    bool "The amplifier enable is active low"
    default n
    help
        The FM8002E's enable line is driven high to play by default. If this
        board wires it through an inverter, or the part is a shutdown-style
        variant where low means run, playing a tone actively mutes the amplifier.

        Symptom is identical to a dead amplifier, so try it before suspecting the
        hardware.

config THERMOPOST_AUDIO_USE_MCLK
    bool "Feed the codec an external master clock"
    default y
    help
        The ES8311 can take MCLK on its own pin or derive it from BCLK. The wiki
        lists MCLK on IO4, so the default assumes it is routed; if it is not, the
        codec never clocks its DAC and stays silent no matter what is written to
        it.

        Turning this off makes the codec derive its clock from BCLK, which works
        on boards that leave the MCLK pin unconnected.

config THERMOPOST_AUDIO_DEBUG
    bool "Dump codec registers and log every tone"
    default n
    help
        Dumps the ES8311's register file after the DAC is powered up, and logs
        each tone's volume, requested decibels and sample count. The register
        dump is the difference between "the codec is not playing" and "the codec
        was never configured", which look the same from outside.

endmenu
